<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suppressions PUBLIC
    "-//Checkstyle//DTD SuppressionFilter Configuration 1.2//EN"
    "https://checkstyle.org/dtds/suppressions_1_2.dtd">

<!--
=============================================================================
config/checkstyle/suppressions.xml
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Purpose:
  The companion file to config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, and the ONLY place in
  this repository where an exemption from the Java documentation gate may be
  recorded. That gate is what mechanically enforces this project's single
  user-specified rule, Rule 1 (Explainability), for Java across all nine Maven
  modules under services/: common-lib, plus the auth, account, card,
  transaction, reference, batch, authorization and reporting services.

  How this file is reached, stated here so it can be understood without opening
  either sibling:
    1. config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml declares a SuppressionFilter whose file
       is ${config_loc}/suppressions.xml and whose optional flag is false.
    2. services/pom.xml supplies config_loc as the directory holding the
       ruleset, and binds maven-checkstyle-plugin to the Maven validate phase
       with failOnViolation true. It deliberately does NOT set the plugin's own
       suppressions-location parameter, so this file is loaded through the
       ruleset's filter and through nothing else.
  Addressing the file relative to the ruleset is what makes it resolve
  identically from the services/ aggregator and from each of the nine module
  directories one level deeper.

  This file MUST exist. It is not a placeholder and it is not optional; see the
  fail-closed note under WHY below for what happens if it is deleted.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Scope (the whole permitted charter):
  Generated sources and test fixtures. Nothing else. Two entries are declared
  below and they are the entire sanctioned surface:
    1. Sources emitted by a build plugin, under services/*/target/.
    2. Fixture material under services/*/src/test/resources/fixtures/.

  Four things must NEVER be added here. Each is restated with its reason in the
  ruled block at the foot of this file, because a prohibition that lives only
  in a header is a prohibition a future editor edits past:
    1. Never hand-written production code under services/*/src/main/java/.
    2. Never a mapper package. A mapper looks generated and is not; the reason
       is recorded at the foot.
    3. Never the test tree as a whole. Tests are IN scope for Rule 1, and
       services/pom.xml sets includeTestSourceDirectory to true precisely so
       that they are audited.
    4. Never a catch-all, meaning a files value of .* or an entry carrying
       neither a files nor a checks attribute.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
How to add an entry:
  Four requirements, all mandatory.
    1. Put the justification in an XML comment IMMEDIATELY ABOVE the element.
       Do not reach for a reason attribute: the DTD has none, and the file
       stops parsing. See the DTD note under WHY below.
    2. Tag that justification with at least one of Rule 1's four categories,
       written in the singular form the house uses in its own files:
       Alternatives Considered, Refactoring Rationale, Assumption, Trade-off.
    3. Narrow the entry with a checks attribute, naming only checks the ruleset
       actually configures. The list is directly below.
    4. Name the broader alternative you rejected AND the concrete consequence
       of having taken it. A justification that does not say what would have
       gone wrong is not a justification.
  Per the contract stated at the foot of checkstyle.xml, each entry answers
  three questions of its own: why the exemption exists at all, how tightly its
  files pattern is scoped, and why the checks it names are the right ones to
  relax rather than a wider family.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Checks available to name, and only these ten:
  JavadocPackage, MissingJavadocType, MissingJavadocMethod,
  MissingJavadocPackage, JavadocType, JavadocMethod,
  NonEmptyAtclauseDescription, SummaryJavadoc, AtclauseOrder,
  CommentsIndentation.

  Assumption: that list is the complete set of checks checkstyle.xml enables,
    and naming anything outside it is worse than useless. A suppression for an
    unconfigured check never fires, so nothing reveals that it is dead, while
    the entry still reads to the next maintainer as evidence that the check is
    active and had to be relaxed. SingleLineJavadoc, JavadocVariable,
    JavadocStyle and WriteTag are the four most likely mistakes here: each is
    deliberately absent from the ruleset, with its reason recorded in that
    file's EXCLUDED MODULES section, so none of them belongs in this one.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
  - Assumption: the justification for every entry sits in an adjacent comment
    because the DTD leaves no alternative. The suppress element is declared
    EMPTY and its attribute list admits exactly six names: files, checks,
    message, id, lines and columns. A reason attribute is not among them, so
    the widely copied blog idiom that writes one onto the element is invalid
    here, and the failure is not a graceful one: the document stops being
    valid against the DTD, and
    because the filter that reads it fails closed, the nine-module reactor
    stops before a single class is compiled. Worse than the breakage is the
    near miss, which is an entry that looks documented while the attribute
    carrying its reason is silently unusable. Upstream Checkstyle keeps the
    justifications for its own suppressions in adjacent comments for this
    reason, and so does this file.
  - Assumption: every entry narrows by checks and none narrows by message.
    Checkstyle's own documentation warns that "filtering by message is
    dependent on runtime locale", and the consequence is specific: a build
    running under a different locale silently stops matching, so an entry
    either stops suppressing what it was written for or starts suppressing
    something adjacent. Check names are stable identifiers and carry no such
    dependency. If a future entry genuinely cannot be expressed any other way,
    it must record that locale exposure next to itself.
  - Trade-off: each checks value spells out all ten check names in full rather
    than abbreviating them to a pattern such as Javadoc.* plus the leftovers.
    The pattern form is shorter and was rejected on two counts. First, a
    wildcard silently absorbs any check added to the ruleset later, so the
    exemption would widen on its own, without an edit to this file and without
    review. Second, matching is performed against the fully qualified check
    class name, so a wildcard's true reach is not readable from the pattern.
    The cost accepted is a long attribute value; what is bought is that every
    alternative in it is literally one of the ten names listed above, which a
    reviewer can check against the ruleset by eye.
  - Trade-off: exactly two entries are declared, and neither is speculative.
    Pre-loading every path the architecture can be predicted to want was
    rejected, because a suppression for a tree that holds no files cannot be
    reviewed: nobody can tell whether it is still needed, whether its pattern
    is as tight as it should be, or whether it has quietly begun matching
    hand-authored code. tests/audit-allowlist.json sets the precedent from the
    other direction, shipping its adjudications list empty on the stated
    ground that each suppression must be an owned decision rather than an
    opaque cutoff. Both entries below are honest about how much work they do.
  - Trade-off: no entry will ever exempt production code, and the reason is
    worth stating as a cost rather than a principle. The tempting use of this
    file is to unblock a build by exempting whatever is failing. The
    consequence of doing that once is that the gate keeps reporting success
    while no longer enforcing Rule 1 for the code it was built for, which is
    strictly worse than having no gate, because a green build is now evidence
    of nothing. Exempting a tree is therefore not available as a remedy; the
    remedy is to write the missing docstring.
  - Assumption: this file failing closed is deliberate and is relied upon from
    two sides. checkstyle.xml sets optional to false, and services/pom.xml
    records that the flag "is relied upon and must not be worked around". With
    optional true and the file absent, Checkstyle's documented behaviour is
    that the filter accepts all audit events: the build would go green having
    suppressed nothing and having verified nothing about which suppressions
    were meant to apply. tests/README.md names that failure mode directly, as
    "a run that verifies NOTHING must not read as green". The practical
    consequence for a future maintainer is narrow and important: emptying this
    file back to a bare suppressions element is correct and supported;
    deleting the file is not, and breaks every build that reads the ruleset.
  - Assumption: the house comment idiom cannot be reproduced literally in XML,
    so the adaptation here is deliberate rather than careless. XML 1.0 section
    2.5 forbids two consecutive hyphens anywhere inside a comment. The house
    files use a hyphen mid-rule and a spaced double hyphen as an em-dash;
    .github/workflows/tests.yml does both. Neither survives here, so the
    mid-rules are tildes and the em-dash is a single hyphen, a colon or a
    semicolon. This is not cosmetic: restoring either one makes the file
    unparseable, and since the filter fails closed, every build that reads it
    fails before a single Java file has been audited. The equals rulers need
    no adaptation and keep the house width of 77.
=============================================================================
  -->

<suppressions>

  <!--
    Generated sources: code emitted by a build plugin, never authored.
      Assumption: this depends on Maven's standard layout, in which a plugin
        writes generated sources beneath target/generated-sources/, and on
        .gitignore ignoring target/ so that nothing matched here is ever
        committed. The pattern is scoped to that one directory segment pair
        rather than to target/ as a whole, so it cannot reach a hand-authored
        tree even if build output is ever redirected.
      Assumption: the exemption is legitimate because Rule 1 governs AUTHORED
        code. The rule asks an author to state a purpose, the parameters, the
        return value and the exceptions; generated code has no author to ask,
        so there is nothing for the gate to police and nothing a contributor
        could do to satisfy it. This is the boundary of the rule's
        applicability, not a relaxation of it, and it mirrors the boundary the
        rule already draws around the reference-only COBOL baseline, which
        requires no retrofitted commentary either.
      Assumption: it names all ten configured checks because a generated file
        would trip the presence checks and the completeness checks alike, and
        CommentsIndentation reports on the placement of whatever comments the
        generator chose to emit. Naming a narrower family would leave the
        remaining checks firing on the same unfixable file.
      Trade-off: this entry is a GUARD, not currently load-bearing, and saying
        so is the point. services/pom.xml binds the gate to the validate
        phase, which runs before generate-sources, so at the moment the audit
        executes no generator has added its output to the compile source roots
        and nothing under target/ is in the audit set. The entry becomes
        load-bearing the moment that stops being true: if the binding moves to
        a later phase, or if the per-service OpenAPI 3.1 contracts are wired
        to generate server stubs or client DTOs whose root is registered by
        then. It is kept for that case rather than removed, because the
        alternative is discovering the need as a build failure with no record
        of why the exemption is correct. What is NOT claimed is that it
        suppresses anything today.
      Alternatives Considered: the same exclusion can be written as an excludes
        entry on maven-checkstyle-plugin in services/pom.xml. Rejected because
        it splits exemption policy across two files: this file would no longer
        be the complete record of what is exempt, and a reviewer reading it
        would draw a false conclusion from its shortness. Both siblings reject
        the mirror image of that split for the same reason, the ruleset
        declining to let the POM name a suppressions file and the POM
        declining to name one.
  -->
  <suppress files="[\\/]target[\\/]generated-sources[\\/]"
            checks="JavadocPackage|MissingJavadocType|MissingJavadocMethod|MissingJavadocPackage|JavadocType|JavadocMethod|NonEmptyAtclauseDescription|SummaryJavadoc|AtclauseOrder|CommentsIndentation"/>

  <!--
    Test fixture material: records derived from the copybook layouts.
      Assumption: fixtures under src/test/resources/fixtures/ are DATA, not
        behaviour. A fixture record is a fixed-width image of an app/cpy/
        copybook layout, and its meaning lives in that layout, at the declared
        record length, rather than in a docstring attached to it. Asking for a
        purpose, parameters and a return value from a 300-byte account image
        has no answer to give.
      Assumption: it names all ten configured checks for the same reason as the
        entry above, so that no member of the family is left firing on
        material that cannot answer it.
      Trade-off: this entry is DEFENSIVE and may never fire, which is stated
        rather than glossed. checkstyle.xml sets fileExtensions to java, so
        the fixed-width fixture files under resources/ are already outside the
        audit set and need no exemption. What remains is the narrow case the
        entry actually covers: a .java file generated into, or co-located
        with, that fixtures directory. It is belt-and-braces for that case. It
        is not load-bearing for the fixture data itself, and must not be
        described as though it were.
      Alternatives Considered: the obvious broader form is a single entry on
        src/test/. Rejected outright, and this is the boundary that matters
        most about this entry: it covers src/test/resources/fixtures/ and must
        never be widened to src/test/java/. Widening it would exempt every
        controller, service and repository test in all nine modules. Those
        tests are where the business rules transcribed from the COBOL are
        actually asserted, which makes their documentation the part a reviewer
        most needs, and the house convention already requires it in terms that
        leave no room: "Every new test, fixture builder, helper, mock, and
        runner routine must carry a docstring stating Purpose, Parameters,
        Returns, and Exceptions". services/pom.xml sets
        includeTestSourceDirectory to true so that those tests are audited; an
        entry on src/test/ would undo that setting from this side while
        leaving it apparently in force.
  -->
  <suppress files="[\\/]src[\\/]test[\\/]resources[\\/]fixtures[\\/]"
            checks="JavadocPackage|MissingJavadocType|MissingJavadocMethod|MissingJavadocPackage|JavadocType|JavadocMethod|NonEmptyAtclauseDescription|SummaryJavadoc|AtclauseOrder|CommentsIndentation"/>

  <!--
=============================================================================
NEVER SUPPRESS: what must not be added to this file, and the consequence.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Alternatives Considered: every item below is a plausible entry that was
  evaluated and rejected. They are written out because the two entries above
  are the readable part of this file and the dangerous part is invisible: an
  exemption nobody wrote. Each item names what adding it would actually cost.
  Nothing here is a suppress element, and nothing here should become one.

1. services/*/src/main/java/ : NEVER, not one entry and not one pattern that
   could match it. This is the hand-authored production code of all nine
   modules and it is the entire population the gate exists to police.
   Exempting any part of it leaves the build reporting success over code that
   Rule 1 no longer reaches, and the report is then indistinguishable from a
   genuine pass. This is the reason config/checkstyle exists as a directory of
   its own rather than as a file tucked under services/.

2. A mapper package : NEVER, and this is the trap most likely to be walked
   into, because the word "mapper" reads as "generated" in most codebases. In
   this one it is the opposite. The mappers are the anti-corruption layer and
   they are hand-written deliberately; MapStruct was evaluated and rejected on
   the recorded ground that copybook-to-DTO mapping "is not mechanical: it
   drops FILLER, masks the primary account number to its last four digits,
   suppresses the card verification value entirely, encrypts the national and
   government-issued identifiers, and renames three misspelled baseline
   fields. Every one of those needs an inline justification at the mapping
   site, which a generated mapper cannot hold." Each of those five behaviours
   is a decision a reader cannot recover from the code, which makes a mapper
   the HIGHEST value documentation target in the repository, not a candidate
   for exemption. checkstyle.xml widens type scope to package for this exact
   population, noting that a mapper "is often package-private precisely
   because it is internal, and internal is where an undocumented type costs a
   future reader most."

3. A service Application class or a config package : NEVER. These are the
   "module entry point" and configuration types that Rule 1's presence clause
   names outright. There is a sibling route to the same exemption and it is
   closed on purpose: checkstyle.xml declines to set skipAnnotations, even
   though the upstream documentation's own example for MissingJavadocType sets
   it to SpringBootApplication and Configuration. Copying that example would
   exempt every service's entry point and every class under every config
   package. Do not reopen from this side what that file closed from its own.

4. The test tree as a whole : NEVER. Tests are in scope for Rule 1, the house
   convention requires a docstring on every test, fixture builder, helper,
   mock and runner routine, and services/pom.xml turns on test-source auditing
   so that the requirement is enforced rather than aspirational. Only
   genuinely mechanical fixture material may be narrowed, which is what the
   second entry above does and the precise reason it stops at resources/.

5. A catch-all : NEVER. A files value of .*, or an element carrying neither a
   files nor a checks attribute, exempts everything from everything. The DTD
   permits it, because every attribute is optional, so nothing but review
   stands in the way. One such element silently disables the whole gate while
   leaving all eleven modules of the ruleset in place and apparently in force,
   which is the most expensive possible outcome for the fewest characters.

6. An in-code bypass : NEVER, and this one cannot be added here at all, which
   is the point. The ruleset configures no SuppressWarningsFilter, no
   SuppressionCommentFilter and no SuppressWithNearbyCommentFilter, so a
   suppression cannot be written into a Java source as an annotation or a
   magic comment. That omission is what gives this file its value: were any of
   the three configured, the gate could be switched off line by line across
   any number of files with no single artifact showing that it had been. Every
   exemption is a durable entry here, and a reviewer can read the complete
   list in one place. Adding one of those filters to the ruleset would take
   that property away, so the request belongs nowhere.

Assumption: the entries above are ordered oldest first, so append new ones
  directly beneath the second entry and above this block. Do not delete the
  enclosing suppressions element when the last entry goes: the file must stay
  present and parseable, because the filter that reads it fails closed.
=============================================================================
  -->

</suppressions>
